<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Subject–Verb Agreement - What it is</h1>
  <p>
    The <strong>subject</strong> tells us who or what the sentence is about. The verb must agree with it in number. In a longer subject, find the <strong>head noun</strong>. Do not let the words beside it choose the verb for you.
  </p>
  <section class="summary-block summary-sand">
    <h2>Singular and plural</h2>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Subject</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">Verb</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Singular</th>
          <td>Often verb + <strong>-s</strong> in the simple present</td>
          <td>The dog <strong>barks</strong>. She <strong>likes</strong> ice cream.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Plural</th>
          <td>Usually the base verb in the simple present</td>
          <td>The dogs <strong>bark</strong>. They <strong>like</strong> ice cream.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The <em>-s</em> changes direction. Plural nouns often end in <em>-s</em> (<em>bird → birds</em>), but a third-person singular present verb often ends in <em>-s</em> (<em>one bird sings; two birds sing</em>).</p>
    <p>The past form <em>played</em> stays the same with a singular or a plural subject.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-yellow">
    <h2>He, she and it take -s</h2>
    <p><strong>He, she</strong> and <strong>it</strong> take a verb with <strong>-s</strong>. <strong>I, you, we</strong> and <strong>they</strong> do not.</p>
    <p>He <strong>plays</strong>. She <strong>walks</strong>. It <strong>bites</strong>.</p>
    <p>I <strong>run</strong>, not “runs”. You <strong>eat</strong>, not “eats”.</p>
    <p>The cat <strong>runs</strong>. A girl <strong>likes</strong> music.</p>
    <p>The cats <strong>run</strong>. My friends <strong>like</strong> music.</p>
  </section>
</div>
